      "id": "paper-v-best-basis-minimum",
      "work": "paper-v",
      "category": "wording",
      "source": {
        "path": "papers/paper-v/current/editable/traditional/Paper_V_Traditional_Final.tex",
        "line": 2446,
        "expected": "with \\(F(v)=c(v)\\) at terminal vertices. Then \\(F(v)\\) is the minimum cost of every admissible pruning of the subtree rooted at \\(v\\). A minimizing pruning is recovered by retaining \\(v\\) when the first term is no larger than the second and splitting it otherwise.",
        "reading_passage": "read/paper-v/admissible-packet-trees.html#L2436"
      },
      "issue": "The phrase minimum cost of every admissible pruning can be read as assigning the same cost to each pruning; the stated recursion minimizes over the feasible prunings.",
      "replace_from": "minimum cost of every admissible pruning",
      "replace_to": "minimum cost among all admissible prunings",
      "existing_clarification": {
        "path": "papers/paper-v/current/editable/traditional/Paper_V_Traditional_Final.tex",
        "line": 2427,
        "expected": "The value at a node is the least cost among all admissible prunings of the subtree rooted there. It is one optimal value. Individual competing prunings may have larger costs. The recursion compares retaining the node with the sum of the already optimal child values, with retention chosen on a tie.",
        "reading_passage": "read/paper-v/admissible-packet-trees.html#L2426"
      },
      "status": "recommended_for_next_manuscript_edition",
      "proposed_source_line": "with \\(F(v)=c(v)\\) at terminal vertices. Then \\(F(v)\\) is the minimum cost among all admissible prunings of the subtree rooted at \\(v\\). A minimizing pruning is recovered by retaining \\(v\\) when the first term is no larger than the second and splitting it otherwise."
    },
